# THE NOETHERVIM LESSONS

Eleven short lessons, run inside the editor, each one ending with something
you have actually done rather than read. Press `<CR>` on a lesson to open
it.

None of them can damage your setup. A tutorial buffer is never written to
disk, so you can edit freely in any of them.

## START HERE

If Neovim itself is new to you, do these two first. They ship with Neovim
and teach modes, motions, editing and saving. Nothing below will make sense
without them, and they are the best half hour you can spend.

    [Vim lesson 1](@tutor:vim-01-beginner)   moving, editing, saving
    [Vim lesson 2](@tutor:vim-02-beginner)   search, replace, files, windows

## THE CORE

Six lessons covering what NoetherVim is, in the order they build on each
other. About an hour in total, and you can stop after any of them.

    1. [Orientation](@tutor:noethervim-01-orientation)
       What you are looking at, and the four questions you can ask the
       editor about itself.

    2. [Keymap namespaces](@tutor:noethervim-02-namespaces)
       The six key prefixes and what each owns. **Do this one first** if
       you do nothing else; it is what makes the rest discoverable.

    3. [Files and windows](@tutor:noethervim-03-files-and-windows)
       The editable directory browser, the pickers, splits and panels.

    4. [Writing and code profiles](@tutor:noethervim-04-profiles)
       Why a `.tex` file and a `.py` file behave differently, and how to
       disagree per window.

    5. [Your configuration](@tutor:noethervim-05-your-config)
       Ends with a configuration file of your own on disk.

    6. [Bundles](@tutor:noethervim-06-bundles)
       Turning subjects on: LaTeX, debugging, git, tests.

## BY SUBJECT

Five more, in any order. Each says at the top which bundle it needs, if
any.

    7. [Language servers and diagnostics](@tutor:noethervim-07-lsp)
       Definitions, references, renames, errors. Core.

    8. [Running code and terminals](@tutor:noethervim-08-terminal)
       The terminal, the run keys, the quickfix list. Core.

    9. [Git](@tutor:noethervim-09-git)
       Hunks and blame are core; the interfaces are the `tools/git` bundle.

   10. [LaTeX](@tutor:noethervim-10-latex)
       Compiling, the PDF, labels and references. Needs `languages/latex`.

   11. [Snippets](@tutor:noethervim-11-snippets)
       Expanding, jumping, and writing your own. Core.

## REFERENCE, ONCE YOU ARE PAST THE LESSONS

A lesson teaches a path through something; these answer a question about it.

    `<Space>?`                  every key active right now, grouped by
                              prefix, with a jump to what defined it
    `:NoetherVim`               every subcommand the distribution adds
    `:checkhealth noethervim`   whether anything is wrong
    `:help noethervim`          the reference manual

There is also a written walkthrough of a first session, outside the editor,
in `docs/onboarding/first-session.md` in the project root.
